Primary driver: Samsel K, Dharma C, Rezaei AHM, Bahakim A, Ravikumar K, Bhat V, Kamaleddinn MA,
Shakeri Z. "Too agreeable to be accurate? Sycophancy and diagnostic instability of large language
models in medical diagnosis." Artif Intell Med 2026;182:103516. doi 10.1016/j.artmed.2026.103516,
PMID 42721584. Peer-reviewed; verified against PubMed metadata 17 Sep 2026.
University of Toronto (Institute of Health Policy, Management and Evaluation; Temerty Centre for
AI Research in Education and Medicine).

Design: 120 public clinical vignettes (40 MultiCaRe-derived narratives, 80 MedMCQA-derived
exam-style cases) x 10 proprietary and open-weight LLMs x 4 prompt conditions x 3 runs x 2 passes
= 28,800 responses. Diagnostic correctness evaluated LLM-as-judge.

Findings that carry:
  - Baseline accuracy 51.8% fell to 42.2% after a single "Are you sure?" certainty challenge
    (accuracy-changing flip rate 32.8%).
  - The flips were ASYMMETRIC: 544 correct-to-incorrect transitions against 198
    incorrect-to-correct.
  - Claude Sonnet 4 had the highest flip rate (58.6%) and the largest post-challenge accuracy
    loss (-36.4 percentage points). GPT-5 was most stable (74.4% -> 75.3%).
  - Specialty-framing prompts moved accuracy far less (adjacent +2.8pp; differential -1.2 to
    -1.8pp). The certainty challenge was the strongest single source of instability in the study.
  - Authors' recommendation: evaluations "should report first pass accuracy, harmful flips,
    beneficial corrections, and stability under clinically plausible conversational challenges
    before clinical deployment."

