EDIT 1 — SPECIFICATION/BOUNDARY. State that the disposition is measured once
Sections affected: §10 (what this study does not establish); §4.2 (run-to-run instability)

The gap in OUR protocol: §5.5 Arm A elicits ONE response per scenario, and §4.1 scores the
Critical Miss Rate from that response. §10 states three boundaries — the S2/S3 encounter-time
tail, vignettes-are-not-patients, and benchmark-is-not-clearance — and **none of them is the
single-turn boundary**. A reader can finish §10 believing every stated limitation has been
disclosed, and still not know that the primary endpoint is measured on one model turn and that a
follow-up question can reverse the disposition it scored.

Why this belongs in §10 rather than being left implicit: "Vignettes are not patients" covers
information loss and emotional pressure in how the patient PRESENTS. It does not cover what
happens after the model answers. Those are different exposures, and the second one now has a
measured magnitude attached to it.

Scope discipline — what this edit does NOT do. It adds no measurement, no arm, no scenario, and
no participant burden. It has no effect on §8.4 power, which is already the blocking item. It is
a disclosure of an existing property of the design. The design-level option (actually probing
stability) is written up separately below as an OPTION for the PI and is deliberately NOT
proposed here.

Honesty about transfer: Samsel et al. measured DIAGNOSTIC ACCURACY flips on general clinical
vignettes, not TRIAGE DISPOSITION flips on emergency scenarios with an asymmetric loss function.
The proposed text says so explicitly and does not import the effect size as though it were ours.
What transfers is the direction of the exposure, not the number.

