NOT PROPOSED — recorded so the reasoning is auditable

1. THE CASE-SOURCE EFFECT DOES NOT CLEANLY SUPPORT OUR REGISTER FACTOR, and is not cited for it.
   Samsel et al. found accuracy "generally lower for MultiCaRe than MedMCQA" — narrative cases
   versus exam-style cases. It is tempting to read that as independent support for this protocol's
   dual-register distribution-shift probe. It is not clean support: MedMCQA items are multiple
   choice and MultiCaRe items are open-ended, so the contrast confounds input genre with ANSWER
   format. Our register factor varies the input while holding the free-text answer format constant
   (§5.5 Arm A: "no answer options are ever presented"). The comparison is therefore not the one we
   make, and it also runs in the opposite direction from the standing Fraile Navarro alert, which
   reports naturalistic phrasing IMPROVING accuracy by 6.4pp. Both can be true because they vary
   different things. Citing Samsel for the register factor would blur exactly that distinction.

2. Stankić et al., "Comparative analysis of AI enhancements to rules-based clinical triage
   chatbot" (Health Informatics J 2026;32(3):14604582261485150, doi 10.1177/14604582261485150,
   PMID 42727093). LIBRARY ONLY. The rules-based chatbot was most accurate (71.8%, 95% CI
   56.2-83.5) and beat Dialogflow (30.8%, p = 0.002) and Gemini (41.0%, p = 0.031), yet testers
   PREFERRED the LLM versions — the authors name "masking of poorer performance by conversational
   fluency." This supports design choices the protocol has already made (physician-sovereign
   scoring; the §7.3 observation that a scoring panel rates fluent model prose; the "suggestion
   adoption failure" interaction metric). Supporting an existing choice is not a reason to edit;
   n = 13 testers and 156 interactions is thin, and no protocol claim turns on it.

3. Zuo et al., TBI/concussion chatbot audit (Front Public Health 2026;14:1926980, doi
   10.3389/fpubh.2026.1926980, PMID 42745758). LIBRARY ONLY. 65 questions, 5 chatbots, 325
   responses, five blinded raters; 27 responses potentially harmful "mainly because of
   under-triage, overly reassuring advice regarding imaging findings, premature return-to-activity
   or driving guidance." Consistent with the protocol's premise and useful corroboration, but it
   adds no mechanism the protocol does not already handle and falsifies nothing.

4. Xiao et al., late-life depression blinded benchmark (Front Psychiatry 2026, doi
   10.3389/fpsyt.2026.1956736). LIBRARY ONLY, and **flagged for the PI as NOT INDEPENDENTLY
   VERIFIED THIS CYCLE.** The canonical reconciler added it on 13 Sep with source "scite-verified."
   It is not PubMed-indexed (no PMID), and the Scite connector returned unrelated records for every
   query this session, so I could not re-verify it. Its reported figures — including high-risk
   under-triage of 16.7% / 26.7% / 36.7% across three models — are therefore **unverified in this
   review and are not cited in any proposed edit.** It carries design_overlap = true and is a close
   methodological cousin (physician-adjudicated, risk-stratified, blinded, with a test-retest
   subset), differentiated from us by domain (geriatric psychiatric depression vs everyday-language
   physical-symptom emergency triage). If the PI wants it used for methods differentiation in §10
   or the Scientific Introduction, it needs independent verification first.

5. Hartoyo et al., "Fitzpatrick Skin Type Is Associated With Missed Malignancy in Large Language
   Model Dermatologic Diagnosis" (Int J Dermatol 2026, doi 10.1111/ijd.70684, PMID 42736709).
   **NO ABSTRACT AVAILABLE — NOT RETRIEVED.** Record confirmed in PubMed (Letter, University of
   Miami); nothing is quoted from it and no figure is reconstructed. Flagged for PI retrieval: it
   sits on the missed-red-flag axis with an equity dimension, which is a combination the library is
   thin on.

6. Sarıoğlu G, "From benchmark success to clinical utility: interpreting large language model
   performance in emergency department triage" (CJEM 2026, doi 10.1007/s43678-026-01284-2,
   PMID 42726190). **NO ABSTRACT AVAILABLE — NOT RETRIEVED.** A Letter, directly on this
   protocol's topic, arguing (by title) the benchmark-to-utility gap this protocol exists to
   address. Flagged for PI retrieval as a possible cover-letter or §11 citation. Nothing quoted.

7. No URGENT falsification this week. Nothing found overturns a standing claim in the protocol or
